EXT. ICE-CAVE MOUTH - GLACIER - DAWN

A blue gash in a field of white. Wind scours the snow into long ripples. Three tiny figures stand at the lip of a frozen lake, backs to the cave.

NADEZHDA "NADIA" VOLKOV-ASANTE, 40s, orange parka, iced eyelashes, drags a sled carrying a sleeping bag. Next to her, IGNATIUS "NAT" BELL-TAKAHASHI, 30s, hood drawn, gloves split at the fingertips, winds a rope around his waist. Dragging up the rear, DESMOND OKONKWO-PARR, 20s, lagging, hobbles on one swollen ankle.

NADIA
Rest stop. Ten minutes.

NAT
We don't have ten.

NADIA
We have what we have.

She drops the sled's handle. He kneels, checking the knot on the sleeping bag, while Desmond lowers himself onto his pack. Nat pulls out a thermos, takes a swallow, hands it to Desmond. Desmond drinks, hands it to Nadia, who sips and stares across the frozen lake at a ridge of dark rock.

NADIA (CONT'D)
That's the pass.

NAT
How far?

NADIA
Six kilometers. Maybe seven.

DESMOND
(quietly)
I can walk.

NAT
You can't.

DESMOND
I can crawl.

NADIA
Nobody's crawling.

She stands, tightens the sled's strap. Nat rises, unwinds the rope, ties one end to the sled and the other to Desmond's pack. Desmond opens his mouth to protest. Nat holds up a hand.

NAT
You're the anchor. Don't argue.

DESMOND
That's backwards.

NAT
Humor me.

Wind gusts, whipping powder across their faces. Nadia pulls up her hood, turns toward the pass, and leans into the haul line. The sled lurches. Nat falls in beside her. Desmond, tethered, drags his foot.

They walk. No one speaks. Their shadows lengthen across the ice.
